<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("metabolism") %>
<% seeds = sweep.fetch(:seeds) %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% bundle = Lab::MetabolismReading::REWARD_BUNDLE %>
<% constants = Lab::MetabolismReading %>
<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Complexity rises at emergence and then stops rising on every fitness-free substrate the
  programme has tested, and
  <%= link_to "from an emerged start", finding_path("complexity-from-an-emerged-start") %> an
  energy economy did not change that. One reading of the plateau is that nothing in the soup
  is worth computing: a byte off the copy path costs its tape nothing and earns it nothing.
  The Metabolism substrate of DESIGN §1.4 changes that one thing. A cell is paid in energy for
  computing arithmetic on inputs the environment hands it. <strong>Once computing pays, do
  replicators climb past the tasks one mutation reaches, to the ones that need a counted
  loop?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The parents are the emerged worlds the
  <%= link_to "from-emerged sweep", experiment_path("from-emerged") %> continued, chosen by
  its rule. Each terminal world runs another <%= epochs %> epochs, seeds
  <%= seeds.first %>–<%= seeds.last %>, in two arms. Both run an economy in which only the cell
  that starts an interaction pays (influx <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("energy_influx"), delimiter: " " %>,
  a stock cap of <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("energy_stock_cap"), delimiter: " " %>, no theft), and both run
  an arithmetic assay every <%= bundle.fetch("task_every") %> epochs. In the
  <strong>reward</strong> arm a credited task pays
  <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("task_reward"), delimiter: " " %> energy a unit. In
  the <strong>no reward</strong> arm it pays nothing, so that world runs byte for byte as if
  the assay were off. A tape is credited with a task when one of its outputs is right on all
  three input cases. ECHO, INC and DEC are one substitution away from a copier. ADD, SUB, NOT,
  DOUBLE and MUL are the loop tasks: in BFF each needs a counted loop.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>, with every number fixed before a child was
  seeded: <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-10-01, "Metabolism: a
  second, labelled substrate that imports an objective", DESIGN §1.3 item 15. Each reward
  child is paired with the no-reward twin of the same parent and seed. Both are read past a
  settling window of <%= number_with_delimiter constants::SETTLING_WINDOW, delimiter: " " %>
  epochs, and a pair with an extinct child is in no test. There are three one-sided sign tests
  at p &lt; <%= constants::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>H-capability</strong>: the last-decile median
    <span class="mono">task_capability</span>, the number of tasks held by a tenth of the
    world.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H-ladder</strong>, the rung-4 question: the same count over the loop tasks,
    <span class="mono">task_capability_loop</span>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H-complexity</strong>: the from-emerged rule, under which the dominant replicator's
    executed instruction count rises when its last-decile median is at least 1.2 times its
    first. It reads only pairs in which neither child relapsed.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Each test is also read without the four parents the design study piloted, and a shown test
  lists any one or two parents whose pairs it cannot do without.
</p>

<% reading = @show.metabolism %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <% [reading.capability, reading.ladder, reading.complexity].each do |test| %>
      <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.hypothesis %> <%= test.outcome_label %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.ladder_climbed? %>
      <strong>Paid computation climbed to a loop task: see the readings below.</strong>
    <% else %>
      <strong>Paid to compute, replicators took the one-step tasks and stopped there.</strong>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every child of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <ul id="metabolism-finding-tests">
    <% [reading.capability, reading.ladder, reading.complexity].each do |test| %>
      <% comparison = test.comparison %>
      <% leaning = reading.leaning(test) %>
      <% unpiloted = reading.unpiloted(test.hypothesis) %>
      <li class="mb-3">
        <strong><%= test.hypothesis %></strong>: <%= comparison.favouring %> pairs favour the
        reward, <%= comparison.against %> the twin and <%= comparison.ties %> tie, of
        <%= comparison.measured_count %> measured<% if comparison.p_value %>, p = <%= p_value_text(comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.outcome_label %></span>.
        <% if reading.unanimous?(test) %>
          Every pair of all <%= reading.parent_count %> parents favours the reward.
        <% else %>
          By parent, <%= leaning.reward %> lean to the reward, <%= leaning.tied %> tie throughout
          and <%= leaning.twin %> lean to the twin.
        <% end %>
        Without the piloted parents: <%= unpiloted.comparison.favouring %> to
        <%= unpiloted.comparison.against %> with <%= unpiloted.comparison.ties %> ties<% if unpiloted.comparison.p_value %>,
        p = <%= p_value_text(unpiloted.comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= unpiloted.badge_class %>"><%= unpiloted.outcome_label %></span>.
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <% if reading.shown_tests.any? %>
    <p id="metabolism-finding-leave-out">
      <% if reading.carried_tests.empty? %>
        No one or two parents carry a shown test: whichever one or two are left out, the rest
        still read p &lt; <%= constants::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>.
      <% else %>
        <% reading.carried_tests.each do |test| %>
          <%= test.hypothesis %> rests on parents
          <%= test.comparison.carried_by.map { |parents| parents.map { |id| "run #{id}" }.join(" and ") }.to_sentence(two_words_connector: " or ", last_word_connector: ", or ") %>.
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored samples at render time, through
    the same reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("metabolism", anchor: "metabolism-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <h2>The ladder</h2>
  <% reward = reading.reward_arm %>
  <% twin = reading.twin_arm %>
  <% income = reading.income_share %>
  <p id="metabolism-finding-ladder">
    Descriptively, counting a task where it held a tenth of a child's world at any sample:
    ECHO in <%= reading.reached(reward, "echo") %> of <%= reward.children.size %> reward
    children, INC in <%= reading.reached(reward, "inc") %> and DEC in
    <%= reading.reached(reward, "dec") %>, against <%= reading.reached(twin, "echo") %>,
    <%= reading.reached(twin, "inc") %> and <%= reading.reached(twin, "dec") %> of
    <%= twin.children.size %> twins. ECHO came first and INC and DEC close behind it; the
    table below gives the median epochs. <%= reading.loop_children(reward) %> reward children
    and <%= reading.loop_children(twin) %> twins reached a loop task, so
    <%= reading.stepping_stones(reward) + reading.stepping_stones(twin) %> climbed one through
    INC or DEC, the stepping stones of Lenski et al. 2003.
    <% capable = reading.capable_twins %>
    <% if capable.any? %>
      At the end of the run
      <%= capable.size == 1 ? "only one twin" : "only #{capable.size} twins" %>,
      <%= safe_join(capable.map { |child| link_to("run #{child.run_id}", run_path(child.run_id)) }, ", ") %>,
      held a task at a tenth of the world.
    <% end %>
    <% if income %>
      Tasks gave a reward cell about <%= percent_value income.mean %> of its income, between
      <%= percent_value income.low %> and <%= percent_value income.high %> across the children,
      an estimate that ignores the stock cap.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/metabolism_reading", reading: reading.report %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No child of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("metabolism") %> is in this database,
    so there is nothing to read here. This page reads the children at render time and states
    the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What a refuted ladder means</h2>
<p>
  On the lab, a read-only count over all 432 000 stored samples of the 108 children found
  <span class="mono">task_capability_loop</span> at 0 in every one. In only one sample did the
  assay credit any loop task at all: DOUBLE, to 1 of the 256 cells it drew. With no loop task in either arm the test
  cannot favour the reward, and the pre-registration reads a tie throughout as refuted, as
  strongly as its power allows. It needed only 5 discordant pairs, and 0 of 54 reward children
  puts a two-sided 95% upper bound of about 0.066 on the rate at which a paid child reaches a
  loop task in this budget, against the pilot's 0.34.
</p>
<p>
  So when computation pays, BFF copiers take the tasks one substitution away and stop. The
  registered reading of that outcome was that the plateau holds for every feature that needs
  several coordinated mutations, and that the suspects are BFF's instruction set and its
  substitution-only mutation. The design study's landscape measurement, recorded in the
  <%= link_to "design record", record_url %> on 2026-10-01 ("The logic assay, the NAND op
  <span class="mono">~</span> and <span class="mono">task_floor</span>") and restated in the
  Logic pre-registration, points at the instruction set. Its numbers are pilot numbers from a
  scratch copy of the engine, not findings. BFF has no two-input data op, so every two-input
  task needs a counted loop, and the only loop a copier owns is its copy loop. No loop solver
  that still copies lay within 3 substitutions of any evolved solver it measured; the nearest
  it built is 5–7 coordinated substitutions away, and no partial kernel on the way computes
  anything the assay pays for. Indels did not shorten the path. If that holds, pay cannot pull
  a population across the gap, because there is nothing to select along it.
</p>
<p>
  H-complexity being shown is consistent with that. Mirroring a solver and stacking the
  one-step tasks adds executed instructions without a loop, so the dominant replicator grows
  more elaborate in 18 pairs against 1. That reads "paid complexity rises", as the
  pre-registration words it, never "open-ended".
</p>

<h2>What comes next</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Logic", experiment_path(Lab.slug_for("logic")) %>, pre-registered as DESIGN
  §1.3 item 16, keeps this substrate's economy, assay and
  reward and imports a primitive as well as an objective: one assay-only NAND byte, with
  Avida's logic tasks as the ladder, so every rung is a composition of NANDs and a lower rung
  is a part of a higher one. It runs on the same parents and asks the question this sweep
  could not reach: whether a soup assembles the deep rungs, XOR and EQU, from paid parts.
</p>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Nothing about rung 4 on Soup.</strong> This substrate imports an objective:
    whatever rose here was paid for by tasks the programme chose. It is never pooled with the
    fitness-free runs, and rung 4 on Soup stays "not shown".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not open-ended.</strong> H-complexity says the dominant replicator executes more
    of its tape under pay. It says nothing about unbounded growth, and the ladder it would
    have to climb stopped at its first rung of loops.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>A small, arithmetic world.</strong> The inputs are 0 to 15, three cases an assay,
    and the ladder is arithmetic because BFF has no bitwise ops. Another task set, a larger
    input domain or a longer budget is untested.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Children are correlated.</strong> Three children share each parent's world, four
    of the parents were piloted, and it is one substrate and one budget. That is why every
    test is read by parent and without the piloted parents.
  </li>
</ul>
